WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.763
FedRAMP-oriented evidence work usually depends on several categories of documentation.

2
00:00:05.763 --> 00:00:13.099
System descriptions explain the purpose, architecture, users, data types, service model, and operating context.

3
00:00:13.099 --> 00:00:17.290
Architecture diagrams show major components and relationships.

4
00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:22.081
Data flow descriptions explain how information moves through the service.

5
00:00:22.081 --> 00:00:27.170
Inventory records identify assets, components, dependencies, and ownership.

6
00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:30.988
These documents establish the factual base for assessment.

7
00:00:30.988 --> 00:00:36.751
Control implementation narratives connect security requirements to the actual service.

8
00:00:36.751 --> 00:00:42.215
Policies and procedures explain management expectations and repeatable processes.

9
00:00:42.215 --> 00:00:46.032
Configuration baselines describe expected secure states.

10
00:00:46.032 --> 00:00:54.640
Incident response plans explain how the organization prepares for, detects, escalates, responds to, and learns from security events.

11
00:00:54.640 --> 00:01:00.628
Contingency and recovery documentation explains resilience and restoration expectations.

12
00:01:00.628 --> 00:01:05.942
Vulnerability and change management procedures show how risk is handled over time.

13
00:01:05.942 --> 00:01:09.984
Operational records are just as important as formal documents.

14
00:01:09.984 --> 00:01:23.981
Change records, vulnerability tickets, access review artifacts, incident records, monitoring outputs, approval records, scan results, and continuous monitoring records show whether processes are being performed.

15
00:01:23.981 --> 00:01:26.226
A policy may say what should happen.

16
00:01:26.226 --> 00:01:29.070
Operational evidence shows what did happen.

17
00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:34.460
Both are needed because control expectations usually require design and operation.

18
00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:37.678
Different documents must tell a consistent story.

19
00:01:37.678 --> 00:01:41.720
The system description should match the architecture diagram.

20
00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:44.265
The diagram should match the inventory.

21
00:01:44.265 --> 00:01:48.007
The inventory should match scan scope and logging sources.

22
00:01:48.007 --> 00:01:51.750
Control narratives should match procedures and evidence.

23
00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:58.561
When documents contradict each other, reviewers have to resolve basic facts before they can evaluate risk.

24
00:01:58.561 --> 00:02:04.998
Consistency is one of the clearest signs that documentation is being maintained with the real system.
